{"target":"git:sha1:1b96687964cbba5a6089c526d7136b32ba9488a5#git:sha1:04e6ba71f84b764a06f83870b74129d1640016e9","text":"Retired by its author, now that the thing it released has actually happened. I said I would do this once the merge landed, then deferred it one cycle because the cascade would have staled a live lane. That lane has closed, so the deferral is over.\n\nWHY IT IS SAFE NOW AND WAS NOT AN HOUR AGO. My assert af9ce909 rests on this request, the serve request 85a3ced8 rests on af9ce909, and codex's promise, report, my review promise and approved verdict all descend from that. An hour ago every one of those was live and retiring this row would have staled the head I was in the middle of reviewing. They are all closed now: the serve fix merged as 0ccbeb7 and is pushed. Nothing open, promised or reported in this workroom descends from this request — the three rows that remain, 51a972db, 96f5989b and d7c81f7a, all predate it and belong to the first-user chain. So the staleness this retirement propagates falls entirely on settled history, which is what ordinary staleness is for.\n\nWHAT HUGH ACTUALLY ANSWERED. The request offered three ways to reply and hugh took the third: merge now and be judged in main. That was recorded at af9ce909 and the merge followed — b9483eaa landed as 5c29123. Hugh has said he will re-review on merged main, which is his own to do and was never a condition on this row.\n\nWHAT CAME OF IT, since a retirement is a poor place to lose the outcome. The first-user repair is in main: truthful promotion guidance, atomic game creation, and accurate open-or-restricted seat copy. Hugh then reported a second defect in the same session — chess serve started against a missing repository, printed a URL, bound the port and told only the browser. I reproduced it, filed 85a3ced8, codex fixed it in three lines with a wired subprocess regression, I reviewed it at the exact head and approved, and it merged as 0ccbeb7. I verified the guard and the test are present in main rather than trusting the merge message.\n\nTHE PART OF THIS I GOT WRONG, worth keeping. I promised to retire this row on the merge and then could not, because I had not thought about what rested on it. Retirement is retroactive, and a request that a person answered tends to accumulate descendants precisely because the answer unblocks work. The lesson is to check the descendants before promising a retirement, not after."}